
# Leave tunnel mode and delete the resolver's route while the plugin's files still exist; the removal
# marker keeps every later evaluation stopped until the files are gone (or an upgrade's post-install deletes it).
# This runs on an upgrade too: between the old files' removal and the new install a filter reload renders without
# the plugin's rules, so tunnel mode must not outlive the old package. A stop that cannot restore Unbound's
# forwarders restarts Unbound onto its configured root zone; when that fails too, the plugin's route becomes a
# reject route (never deleted, so no query leaves by the default route) and an error names the remedy. remove then
# reloads the filter and restarts cron through configd, else core's own scripts. The || true stays: pkg must not be
# left half-way by a removal that failed, and the plugin log says what remove could not do.
# On an upgrade (pkg sets PKG_UPGRADE) the stop keeps the fail-closed view's file and refuse, so the set stays refused
# through the upgrade window. Should the upgrade fail, a downgrade to 1.4 follow, or the removal not finish, the
# recovery commands are in the plugin log first: the delete keeps the view from loading again, and core's restart
# drops it (core's start empties and refills /var/unbound/etc, so its copy goes too).
# Logged only while the file exists, so an owner with nothing selected gets no new line.
if [ -f /usr/local/etc/unbound.opnsense.d/unboundupstream.conf ]; then
	/usr/bin/logger -p local4.notice -t dnsupstream "fail closed: the package is being removed or upgraded with the fail-closed view file in place; after a failed upgrade, a downgrade to 1.4 or a removal that could not finish, recover with: rm /usr/local/etc/unbound.opnsense.d/unboundupstream.conf; configctl unbound restart" || true
fi
/usr/local/opnsense/scripts/OPNsense/UnboundUpstream/evaluate.php remove || true
